WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:04.180
Hey Crazies.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.000
In our last video about light, we talked a
little bit about fusion and I said this:

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.680
Every four protons comes together to make one helium nucleus

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.020
but four protons has more mass than one helium nucleus.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.900
Then people started asking questions in the
comments and I was like:

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.620
Why don’t we just make a video about it?

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:20.720
So let’s do it!

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.680
First, when we say Fusion,
we specifically mean nuclear fusion.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.200
As in, the nucleus of an atom.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.060
Originally, the word “nucleus” meant the
kernel at the center of a nut,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.080
but we’ve since made it mean: the center
of anything small.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.480
Big things have a core.

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:36.140
Small things have a nucleus.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.260
It’s that simple.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.460
Yeah, but what’s fusion?

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.460
Oh right, I guess I kind of skipped that,
didn’t I?

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.320
Fusion means “to combine multiple things
into one thing.”

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.020
With nuclear fusion, smaller atomic nuclei
combine to form larger ones.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.380
Usually it happens in the core of a star,
but it did happen a little in the early universe.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.840
It can also happen in hydrogen bombs and fusion
reactors.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.840
Basically, anywhere with similar conditions
to the core of a star.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.500
So what kind of stuff can we make?

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.120
That depends on what you’re combining?

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:07.130
The Sun is fusing hydrogen into helium, which
shouldn’t be that surprising

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.940
since the Sun is mostly Hydrogen.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.440
Then again, so is the regular matter in the
universe.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.880
Just to be clear though, when we say “hydrogen,”
we don’t mean a hydrogen gas molecule.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.140
or a hydrogen atom.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.680
We mean a hydrogen nucleus.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.160
It’s so hot inside the Sun, the hydrogen
can’t hold onto its electrons anymore.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.400
I'm free!!

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.660
It’s just a bunch of electrons and protons
zipping around freely.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.500
We call this a Plasma.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.020
Oh! Oh! Like the stuff in my blood?

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:31.980
No.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.140
That’s biology.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.580
This is physics.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.040
Totally different things!

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.509
Anyway, those protons are still called “Ionized
Hydrogen” or H plus,

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:43.340
which, as you can imagine, leads to all sorts
of confusion.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.600
Just keep in mind for the rest of the video,
whenever I refer to something on the periodic

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.140
table, I only mean its nucleus.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.360
OK, so why doesn’t fusion just happen everywhere?

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.479
Oh, good question!

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:58.659
You can slap neutral atoms together all day,
but atomic nuclei are charged

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:01.340
and similar charges don’t like to be near
each other.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:06.520
Whether you’re talking giant uranium, middlist
iron, or little tiny hydrogen

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.500
All nuclei carry a positive charge, which
will repel other positive charges.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.540
It’s called the “Coulomb Barrier” and
you need a really strong force to overcome it.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.760
Like, oh I don’t know, maybe gravity!

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.180
The Sun has a lot of that to spare.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.030
I mean, it does contain 99.8% of the solar
system’s mass.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.220
A lot of mass means a lot of gravity.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:33.020
So the Sun, a giant cloud of mostly protons,
uses its own gravity to force those protons

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:34.040
together.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.340
Hulk Smash.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:37.360
Doesn’t helium have neutrons in it though?

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.489
I was wondering how long it was going to take
you to ask about that.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:43.050
So yeah, confession time.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:48.820
While the idea of nuclear fusion is simple,
the actual processes are not,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.040
which is pretty typical of real life, now
that I think about it.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.720
Anyway, before we get into the details, there
are some things you need to know.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.760
First, during all nuclear reactions, energy,
momentum, and charge are all conserved.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.200
Rest mass is not conserved, but it is a type of energy,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.530
so it’s accounted for that way.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.340
We’ll call it rest-energy for the remainder
of the video.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.340
Second, neutrons can decay into protons, whenever
they want,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.040
because neutrons have more rest-energy than
protons.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.000
It doesn’t happen a lot, otherwise there
wouldn’t be any neutrons,

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.060
but it does happen.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.740
Third, protons can decay into neutrons, but
only with some help.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.459
The extra rest-energy has to come from somewhere.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.060
Usually, the kinetic energy lost during a
collision with another particle.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.500
And finally, fourth, remember that protons 
don’t like to be together,

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.540
but neutrons can help keep them together.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.260
Near the top of the Periodic Table, the number
of each is pretty even,

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.060
but, as you move down the table, you need
more neutrons than protons.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.820
Alright,
now we’re ready for some nuclear reactions.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:49.280
We said before Hydrogen fusion turns 4 protons
into 1 Helium and some light,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.040
but some of that light comes from antimatter annihilation with electrons.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.200
There are also a couple neutrinos that
get made

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.360
Because why not?!
and this whole process can happen a couple

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.420
different ways.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.799
The Proton-Proton Chain adds protons one at
a time

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:07.060
creating neutrons along the way through decay
and releasing light and neutrinos that zip

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.060
out of the Sun.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.580
The CNO Cycle releases all the same light
and neutrinos,

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.180
but uses Carbon, Nitrogen, and Oxygen along
the way to build Helium.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.420
Either way, the Sun will eventually run low
on Hydrogen

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.440
forcing it to fuse Helium into Carbon,
which it will do using The Triple-Alpha Process.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.340
In case you’re wondering, Helium nuclei
are sometime called alpha particles

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.480
and it takes 3 of them to make 1 Carbon,
hence “Triple-Alpha.”

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.820
Even after you get the temperature right,
a lot of other things still have to go a certain way.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.980
If you smash 2 protons together and neither
of them decays into a neutron,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.400
then they just fall back apart.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.940
If you smash 2 Heliums together, the third
one better get there soon

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.840
or the first 2 will just fall back apart.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.450
And if you want to make even bigger nuclei,
things just get even more difficult.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.140
The bigger a nuclei is, the more it wants
to repel other nuclei.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.340
Sometimes you can get the core of the star hot
enough for that,

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:02.760
but other times you have sneak in a neutron
and wait for it to decay into a proton.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.830
It’s called “Neutron Capture” and it
can happen either slowly or rapidly.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:11.100
The point is, making all the elements on the
periodic table is harder than it sounds,

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.960
but, if we can it figure out, we’ve got
a great source of clean energy.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.520
So, are you excited about the future of fusion
power?

